          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the pathogenesis of allergic rhinitis, what primarily mediates the late-phase</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the pathogenesis of allergic rhinitis, what primarily mediates the late-phase nasal congestion that develops 4–8 hours after allergen exposure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Histamine released during mast-cell degranulation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Histamine drives the early-phase reaction within minutes, causing itching, sneezing, and rhinorrhoea — not the delayed congestion that follows hours later.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Eosinophilic infiltration and newly synthesised inflammatory mediators</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The late-phase response is characterised by eosinophil recruitment and production of leukotrienes and prostaglandins, which cause sustained nasal mucosal oedema and the stubborn congestion between exposures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IgG-mediated complement activation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allergic rhinitis is an IgE-mediated process. IgG and complement activation are not involved in this type I hypersensitivity pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutrophilic infiltration triggered by bacterial superinfection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Neutrophilic infiltration is characteristic of bacterial infection. The late-phase allergic response is driven by eosinophils, not neutrophils.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The late-phase response (4–8 hours) is mediated by eosinophils and newly synthesised inflammatory mediators, causing the persistent nasal congestion — distinct from the histamine-driven early phase."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The late phase is eosinophil-driven, which is why nasal congestion lingers long after the initial sneezing and itching subside."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
