        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 319.75,
            "to": 329.75
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which examination finding in a child with chronic nasal symptoms most reliably d</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which examination finding in a child with chronic nasal symptoms most reliably distinguishes allergic rhinitis from a viral upper respiratory infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Red, inflamed nasal turbinates</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Red, erythematous turbinates are the hallmark of viral rhinitis. In allergic rhinitis, the turbinates have a distinctly different appearance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Purulent nasal discharge with fever</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Purulent discharge and fever suggest bacterial infection. Allergic rhinitis produces clear, watery rhinorrhoea without systemic signs of infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pale, bluish-grey, boggy nasal turbinates</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pale, oedematous turbinates reflect chronic eosinophilic inflammation of the nasal mucosa — a hallmark finding that distinguishes allergic rhinitis from the erythematous turbinates of viral infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Unilateral nasal obstruction with blood-stained discharge</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Unilateral obstruction with epistaxis raises concern for a nasal foreign body or structural pathology, not allergic rhinitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Allergic rhinitis causes pale, bluish-grey, boggy turbinates due to chronic eosinophilic oedema — in contrast to the red, inflamed turbinates seen with viral infections."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well identified. The pale, boggy turbinates are a key examination clue pointing to allergic rather than infective aetiology."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
