        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 510.9,
            "to": 520.9
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child with persistent allergic rhinitis shows improved asthma control after st</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child with persistent allergic rhinitis shows improved asthma control after starting intranasal corticosteroids, with no changes to inhaled asthma therapy. Which explanation best accounts for this observation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treating upper-airway inflammation reduces the mediators and reflexes that prime the lower airway</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The 'one airway, one disease' principle: the same IgE/Th2 inflammation extends from nose to bronchi. Reducing nasal mucosal inflammation limits aspirated inflammatory mediators and neurogenic reflexes that sustain bronchial hyperreactivity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intranasal corticosteroids are absorbed systemically and directly suppress bronchial inflammation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Intranasal steroids act locally on the nasal mucosa. Systemic absorption is negligible at standard doses and cannot account for improved bronchial outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The improvement is coincidental, since nasal and bronchial allergic disease are pathologically unrelated</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nasal and bronchial allergic disease share the same IgE-mediated Th2 inflammatory pathway — they are continuous, not separate entities.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nasal steroids eliminate post-nasal drip, removing the sole trigger for the child's cough</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While post-nasal drip can contribute, the improvement in bronchial hyperreactivity reflects reduced shared inflammation across the entire airway, not merely removal of one mechanical trigger.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The 'one airway, one disease' principle explains this: IgE/Th2 inflammation is continuous from nose to bronchi, so treating upper-airway inflammation reduces inflammatory priming of the lower airway."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The continuous airway shares one inflammatory pathway, so treating the upper end directly benefits the lower end — the core of the 'one airway, one disease' concept."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
